# Sprint 5 — Attendance (Grade-Based)

## Overview

Sprint 5 builds attendance, but not as a single mechanism for everyone. Facial recognition applies only to secondary students (grades 8–12). Primary students (grades 1–7) get a traditional manual roll-call register instead — deliberately avoiding a biometric, workplace-style check-in for young children, while giving secondary students something closer to a professional check-in environment as preparation for the world beyond school.

## Update — per-period check-in

After the initial build, the secondary flow was changed from one arrival scan and
one departure scan per day to a **per-period, pass-the-device loop**: the teacher
carries the device round the room and scans each student at every class change.
This needs no class/period/timetable structure — only a record of each scan.

- **`AttendanceScan`** logs every check-in (student, the day's `Attendance`,
  timestamp, method, recorded-by). Many per student per day.
- **`Attendance` is now a rollup** for secondary learners: `arrived_at` is the
  first scan of the day, `departed_at` the most recent — so `departed_at` only
  becomes a true departure retroactively, once no later scan arrives. No "last
  scan" flag; the rollup handles it.
- **The manual fallback simplified**: every scan is just a period check-in, so a
  failed match records a manual-method `AttendanceScan` like any other, with no
  arrival/departure distinction to decide.
- **Primary roll-call is unaffected** — still one presence tick per day, no scans.
- Only a teacher initiates each capture; there is no student-triggered scan path.

Done in this update:

- [x] Each face or manual check-in during the day creates an `AttendanceScan`
- [x] `arrived_at` reflects the first scan, `departed_at` the most recent
- [x] Many scans per student per day, without violating one-`Attendance`-row-per-day
- [x] The check-in UI is a pass-the-device loop: capture, confirm, reset, next
