## Goals

- Add a `grade` field to `User` so attendance can route by grade band
- Build a manual roll-call register for primary students (1–7)
- Build facial-recognition check-in/check-out for secondary students (8–12), with consent and a manual fallback
- Land both mechanisms on one shared `Attendance` model, so later sprints don't need to know which method produced a record
- Enforce grade-based routing at the view level, not just in the UI

## What was built

- **`User.grade`** — integer field, 1–12, added to the existing student registration form from Sprint 1 as a required field for students. A small addition to existing auth, not new auth work.
- **`Attendance` model** — student (FK), date, `method` (`"manual"` / `"facial"`), `arrived_at` (nullable), `departed_at` (nullable). One record per student per day, enforced at the database level. For secondary learners it is a rollup of the day's `AttendanceScan`s (see the per-period update above). Viewable and markable by any teacher — not scoped to whoever created the record, since this is a shared front-desk function, not personal marking work.
- **Primary flow (grades 1–7)** — a register view listing all grade 1–7 students (no class/roster structure yet, same known limitation as Sprints 3/4). Teacher ticks who's present, submits; creates `Attendance(method="manual", arrived_at=now)` per student for today. No `departed_at` tracking — a register records presence, not clock-in/clock-out. A student with no record for the day is implicitly absent.
- **`FaceEnrollment` model (secondary only)** — student (FK), stored face descriptor (never the source photo), enrolled-by, enrolled-at, and a `consent_confirmed` boolean the enrolling teacher must explicitly check
- **Enrollment flow** — teacher selects a secondary student, captures a reference photo via browser camera, confirms consent, saves. Re-enrolling updates the existing descriptor rather than duplicating it.
- **`AttendanceScan` model (secondary)** — one row per check-in event: student, the day's `Attendance` (FK), timestamp, `method`, `recorded_by`. Many per student per day (see the per-period update above).
- **Per-period check-in flow (secondary)** — the teacher scans each student in turn; each scan appends an `AttendanceScan` via one shared `record_scan`, and the day's `Attendance` rolls up (`arrived_at` = first scan, `departed_at` = most recent). `method="facial"` on a match.
- **Shared scan path** — a failed facial match records a manual-method `AttendanceScan` through the same `record_scan`, so there is one per-period path, not a duplicated manual-entry one. (Primary roll-call keeps its own presence-only `mark_present_manually`, unchanged.)
- **Grade-based routing enforced server-side** — a grade 1–7 student is never offered facial enrollment; a grade 8–12 student doesn't appear in the manual roll-call list except via the fallback path. Enforced at the view level based on `User.grade`, not hidden UI.
- **Attendance history** — a list viewable by any teacher, showing who's been marked present today across both methods, with the method that produced each record

## Implementation notes

Decisions worth having on record, including two I want to flag explicitly.

- **The face match runs server-side, not in the browser.** The spec reads
  "face-api.js matches against enrolled descriptors", which could be taken as
  client-side matching. I did it the other way: the browser uses face-api.js
  only to turn a camera frame into a 128-float descriptor, posts that, and the
  comparison happens on the server (`attendance/matching.py`, euclidean distance,
  face-api.js's own 0.6 threshold). Two reasons — these are children's biometric
  vectors, and client-side matching would mean shipping every enrolled
  descriptor to whatever device runs the check-in; and server-side matching is
  testable without a browser. The check-in station is teacher-operated, so a
  teacher-supplied descriptor is an acceptable trust boundary. Flagging because
  it is a real deviation from a literal reading.

- **Per-period rollup (post-build refinement).** The day's `Attendance` for a
  secondary learner is derived from its `AttendanceScan`s: the first sets
  `arrived_at`, every scan moves `departed_at` to its own time. `departed_at` is
  therefore "most recent scan so far", a true departure only once the day ends
  with no later scan — no flag marks the last one. This also resolved an earlier
  flag about manual departure: with every scan being just a period check-in, the
  fallback needs no arrival/departure distinction at all.

- **Two clean paths, not one muddled one.** Primary roll-call uses
  `mark_present_manually` (a presence tick, no scans). Every secondary per-period
  check-in — facial or manual fallback — uses `record_scan`, which appends a scan
  and rolls up the day. Each path exists once; the fallback and the facial flow
  share `record_scan`, so a no-match is genuinely the same code as a match, only
  with `method="manual"`.

- **Grade routing is enforced in four places, all server-side.** Enrollment: the
  form queryset is secondary-only *and* `FaceEnrollment.clean()` refuses a
  primary student. Roll-call: the list is primary-only *and* the POST handler
  filters submitted ids against the primary set, so a tampered secondary id does
  nothing. The fallback is secondary-only. None of it relies on hidden UI.

- **Only the descriptor is stored.** `FaceEnrollment` has no photo field by
  design; the reference image is used in the browser and discarded. Consent is a
  required boolean the enrolling teacher must tick, enforced by the form and the
  model.

- **Absence stays implicit, as the spec intended.** A student with no row for a
  day was not marked present; there is no "absent" state. Built as specified —
  it reads fine to me, so no change proposed.

- **`User.grade`** is nullable on the model (teachers and parents have none) and
  required for students by the registration form, not the database — a small
  addition to Sprint 1's auth, not a rebuild.

